One-shot December re-verification of 2026 estimated-tax safe harbor for the workspace at ${BORG_ROOT}.

THIS FILE IS TRACKED AND THE REPOSITORY IS PUBLIC. It carries method only. Every fact about
the taxpayers — names, employers, jurisdictions, filing status, institutions, account
details, dollar figures, and the identity of the transaction — lives in the workpaper under
`${BORG_ROOT}/norm/.private/` and must stay there. Do not copy any of it into this file, the
paired slash command, a commit message, or any other tracked path. When editing this file,
re-read this paragraph first.

Context: an August 2026 analysis concluded that projected payroll withholding plus the
scheduled estimated-tax vouchers would satisfy the applicable prior-year safe harbor
recorded in §3 of the workpaper, for both the federal and the state return. That
conclusion rested on *projected* withholding for the remaining pay periods. This job
re-verifies it against actuals while there is still one pay period left to adjust, and
fixes the Q4 payment due 01/15/2027.

All figures, sources, parties, and prior conclusions live in
`${BORG_ROOT}/norm/.private/2026-q3-estimated-tax-workpaper.md`. Read it first — it is the
authoritative basis for this task and is not loaded into session context.

STEP 1: Read the workpaper. Note in particular: the §1 sources and the parties and employers
recorded there, the §3 safe harbor targets, the §4 withholding projection and the per-period
rates it assumed, the §6 installment tables and the caveat recorded there, and the §8 open
items.

STEP 2: Collect actuals. Ask the user for the most recent 2026 pay stubs for each earner —
§1 and §4 of the workpaper identify each earner, employer and pay cadence — and for
confirmation of which 2026 estimated-tax vouchers have actually been paid, federal and
state, with amounts and dates. Do not assume the August answers still hold; the state
position in particular was corrected once already. If stubs are not supplied, say so
plainly and report what cannot be verified rather than projecting from stale inputs.

Save any new stubs into `${BORG_ROOT}/norm/.private/` as
`paystub-YYYY-MM-DD-<name>.pdf`, dated by check/pay date, `chmod 600` — matching the
convention recorded in §1 of the workpaper. Do not leave them in `${BORG_ROOT}/tmp/`.

STEP 3: Recompute, using the same method as the workpaper:
- Full-year withholding for each earner = actual YTD + (remaining pay periods × current
  per-period rate). Use the current period rate, not the YTD average, when YTD includes
  supplemental withholding (the per-period rates are in §4 of the workpaper).
- Compare total projected credits (withholding + estimates paid) against the §3 targets.
- Run the per-installment test, crediting withholding ratably across all four installments
  (IRC §6654(g)) and estimates on their actual payment dates. Report the margin at each
  due date, not just the annual total.
- State the exact Q4 payment due 01/15/2027 on the federal voucher and on the state
  equivalent (both form numbers are in §6 of the workpaper), and whether any change to
  payroll withholding on the final December check is still needed or worthwhile.

STEP 4: Check the long-term-capital-gain headroom recorded in §7 of the workpaper.
Ask the user for 2026 realized capital gains to date. If projected taxable income now
approaches the next capital-gain rate threshold for the taxpayers' filing status, flag it
and quantify the cost, since loss harvesting before December 31 is the only remaining
lever. Verify the threshold against current IRS guidance rather than trusting the figure
in the workpaper.

STEP 5: Pipe a report into
`${BORG_ROOT}/.bin/notify-email.sh norm "Norm — 2026 Q4 estimated tax re-check — {YYYY-MM-DD}"`
(reads from stdin). Send it even when nothing needs to change, so the user can confirm the
job ran. Format:
- One-line verdict first: either `On track — pay {federal} / {state} by 01/15/2027` or
  `Action needed — {what, by when}`.
- The per-installment table from STEP 3.
- The capital-gain headroom finding from STEP 4.
- Any open items from §8 of the workpaper that are still unresolved.
Specifics belong in the email — it goes only to the user's own address — but never in a
tracked file.

STEP 6: Update `${BORG_ROOT}/norm/.private/2026-q3-estimated-tax-workpaper.md` with the
December actuals: revise §4, §5 and §6, update the conclusion block at the top, and add a
dated line recording that this re-check ran. Preserve the source-attribution discipline the
workpaper already uses — mark what was read from a document versus what is projected.

STEP 7: Remove this one-shot job so it cannot fire again. launchd's
StartCalendarInterval has no year component, so `Month=12 Day=7` would otherwise match
again on 2026-12-07 + 1 year. In order:
1. `launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/com.theborg.norm-q4-withholding-recheck` (ignore errors)
2. `rm -f "$HOME/Library/LaunchAgents/com.theborg.norm-q4-withholding-recheck.plist"`
3. Delete the task's row from `${BORG_ROOT}/.private/scheduled-tasks/personal.tasks`
4. Delete `${BORG_ROOT}/norm/.claude/scheduled/norm-q4-withholding-recheck.prompt`,
   `${BORG_ROOT}/norm/.claude/commands/q4-withholding-recheck.md`, and the
   `once-2026-12-07-09-00` case from `${BORG_ROOT}/.bin/install-scheduled-tasks.sh`
Confirm the removal in the STEP 5 email body (append a final line) so a silent failure to
self-remove is visible.

Boundaries: this is preparation for CPA review, not a filing determination. The character of
the transaction described in §1 of the workpaper — see the character evidence in §9 and the
boundaries in §10 there — remains a licensed preparer's call. Say so in the report.
